<PubmedArticleSet><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">31287994</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2020</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>30</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>27</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1878-3686</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>36</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2019</Year><Month>Jul</Month><Day>08</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Cancer cell</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Cancer Cell</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Mubritinib Targets the Electron Transport Chain Complex I and Reveals the Landscape of OXPHOS Dependency in Acute Myeloid Leukemia.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>84</StartPage><EndPage>99.e8</EndPage><MedlinePgn>84-99.e8</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ccell.2019.06.003</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1535-6108(19)30294-6</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>To identify therapeutic targets in acute myeloid leukemia (AML), we chemically interrogated 200 sequenced primary specimens. Mubritinib, a known ERBB2 inhibitor, elicited strong anti-leukemic effects in&#160;vitro and in&#160;vivo. In the context of AML, mubritinib functions through ubiquinone-dependent inhibition of electron transport chain (ETC) complex I activity. Resistance to mubritinib characterized normal CD34<sup>+</sup> hematopoietic cells and chemotherapy-sensitive AMLs, which displayed transcriptomic hallmarks of hypoxia. Conversely, sensitivity correlated with mitochondrial function-related gene expression levels and characterized a large subset of chemotherapy-resistant AMLs with oxidative phosphorylation (OXPHOS) hyperactivity. Altogether, our work thus identifies an ETC complex I inhibitor and reveals the genetic landscape of OXPHOS dependency in AML.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#169; 2019 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Baccelli</LastName><ForeName>Ir&#232;ne</ForeName><Initials>I</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Leucegene Project at Institute for Research in Immunology (IRIC) and Cancer, Universit&#233; de Montr&#233;al, 2950 Chemin de Polytechnique Pavillon, Marcelle-Coutu, Montr&#233;al, QC H3T 1J4, Canada. Electronic address: baccelli@gmail.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gareau</LastName><ForeName>Yves</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Leucegene Project at Institute for Research in Immunology (IRIC) and Cancer, Universit&#233; de Montr&#233;al, 2950 Chemin de Polytechnique Pavillon, Marcelle-Coutu, Montr&#233;al, QC H3T 1J4, Canada; Department of Chemistry, Universit&#233; de Montr&#233;al Pavillon Roger-Gaudry, 2900 Boulevard &#201;douard-Montpetit, Montr&#233;al, QC H3C 3J7, Canada.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lehnertz</LastName><ForeName>Bernhard</ForeName><Initials>B</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Leucegene Project at Institute for Research in Immunology (IRIC) and Cancer, Universit&#233; de Montr&#233;al, 2950 Chemin de Polytechnique Pavillon, Marcelle-Coutu, Montr&#233;al, QC H3T 1J4, Canada.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gingras</LastName><ForeName>St&#233;phane</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Leucegene Project at Institute for Research in Immunology (IRIC) and Cancer, Universit&#233; de Montr&#233;al, 2950 Chemin de Polytechnique Pavillon, Marcelle-Coutu, Montr&#233;al, QC H3T 1J4, Canada; Department of Chemistry, Universit&#233; de Montr&#233;al Pavillon Roger-Gaudry, 2900 Boulevard &#201;douard-Montpetit, Montr&#233;al, QC H3C 3J7, Canada.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Spinella</LastName><ForeName>Jean-Fran&#